  <h2 class="section-title">Biographical Sketches for the USA Participants</h2>
  <p>Two-page NSF Curriculum Vitae for Ann Christy and Andy Ward are included in the proposal together with contact details for all the participants. Biographical sketches have not been included for the African partners as invitations to them will only be made when the grant is awarded.</p>

  <p><span data-field="bio_summary">{{ bio_summary }}</span></p>
  <p><em>"Before I participated in that initiative, I thought that engineering was the ultimate solution to the world's problems; develop a technology, and the rest will fall into place. However, after my experience in South Africa, I realized that the problems are much more complex and that the solutions must be context-specific. I realized how unaware of the impoverished people in South Africa are of the rest of the world. I realized that the complex problems of poverty, lack of infrastructure, and social inequality are not just engineering problems, but are deeply intertwined with economic, environmental, and societal context. Most of all, I realized that even small steps by university students can make a difference. My senior capstone team designed a sustainable and cost-effective water purification system for a rural community in South Africa. This project taught us that engineering is not just about building things, but about understanding the people we are trying to help. This design project did not focus on impoverished communities, I still left my peers and me with a broader sense of responsibility to address the complex issues that exist in the world. We learned that engineering is a tool to help people, but it is not a magic wand that can solve everything. We must be humble and recognize the constraints of developing countries."</em></p>

